!type: Basic Math
!deck: Math
!tags: Math Logic Set-Builder

!id: sR5Kufdkb5
!front: In set-builder notation, what are the three main pieces of \( \{x \in U : P(x)\} \)?
!back:
- \( x \) is the bound variable, ranging over possible elements.
- \( U \) is the universe, or ambient set, from which candidates are drawn.
- \( P(x) \) is the predicate that decides which candidates are included.

The set contains exactly those \( x \in U \) for which \( P(x) \) is true.
~~~

!id: SBddjkIEpm
!front: Write in set-builder notation: the real numbers within distance \( \varepsilon \) of \( a \), where \( a \in \mathbb{R} \) and \( \varepsilon > 0 \).
!back: \[ \{x \in \mathbb{R} : |x-a| < \varepsilon\} \]
~~~

!id: VwshM50fa0
!front: Write in set-builder notation: the integers divisible by \( 3 \), but not divisible by \( 9 \).
!back: \[ \{n \in \mathbb{Z} : 3 \mid n \text{ and } 9 \nmid n\} \]
!related: Equivalently, \( \{3k : k \in \mathbb{Z} \text{ and } 3 \nmid k\} \).
~~~

!id: 71y0eKF97b
!front: Write in set-builder notation: the rational numbers in \( [0,1] \), represented in lowest terms with positive denominator.
!back: \[ \left\{\frac{p}{q} : p,q \in \mathbb{Z},\ q>0,\ 0 \le p \le q,\ \gcd(p,q)=1\right\} \]
!extra: The conditions \( q>0 \) and \( \gcd(p,q)=1 \) make the representation canonical.
~~~

!id: wXas4xdZdj
!front: Write in set-builder notation: the points of \( \mathbb{R}^2 \) strictly inside the unit disk but on neither coordinate axis.
!back: \[ \{(x,y) \in \mathbb{R}^2 : x^2+y^2<1,\ x \ne 0,\ y \ne 0\} \]
~~~

!id: ewJGRoRy76
!front: Write in set-builder notation: the closed annulus in \( \mathbb{R}^2 \) between radii \( r \) and \( R \), where \( 0 \le r \le R \).
!back: \[ \{(x,y) \in \mathbb{R}^2 : r^2 \le x^2+y^2 \le R^2\} \]
~~~

!id: DFxmJgWcAZ
!front: For functions \( g,h : \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R} \), write the natural domain of \( f(x)=\dfrac{\sqrt{g(x)}}{h(x)-1} \) in set-builder notation.
!back: \[ \{x \in \mathbb{R} : g(x) \ge 0 \text{ and } h(x) \ne 1\} \]
!extra: The square root requires a nonnegative radicand, and the denominator must be nonzero.
~~~

!id: yoXSK7Tn53
!front: For \( f : A \to B \), write the range, or image, of \( f \) in set-builder notation.
!back: \[ f(A)=\{y \in B : \exists x \in A,\ y=f(x)\} \]
!extra: Using \( y \in B \) makes the codomain explicit; the existential quantifier says \( y \) is actually hit by some input.
~~~

!id: pbSBhm4Pae
!front: For \( f : A \to B \) and \( S \subseteq A \), write the image of \( S \) under \( f \) in set-builder notation.
!back: \[ f(S)=\{y \in B : \exists x \in S,\ y=f(x)\} \]
!related: Another common notation is \( \{f(x) : x \in S\} \).
~~~

!id: WtxPQcRxGy
!front: For \( f : A \to B \) and \( T \subseteq B \), write the preimage of \( T \) under \( f \) in set-builder notation.
!back: \[ f^{-1}(T)=\{x \in A : f(x) \in T\} \]
!extra: This does not require \( f \) to have an inverse function. The notation names a subset of the domain.
~~~

!id: oHLTeOfngy
!front: For \( f : A \to B \), write the graph of \( f \) in set-builder notation.
!back: \[ \operatorname{graph}(f)=\{(x,y) \in A \times B : y=f(x)\} \]
!related: Equivalently, \( \{(x,f(x)) : x \in A\} \).
~~~

!id: XAuDuV3yCn
!front: For \( f : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R} \) and \( c \in \mathbb{R} \), write the level set where \( f=c \) in set-builder notation.
!back: \[ \{x \in \mathbb{R}^n : f(x)=c\} \]
~~~

!id: CGoIFIYpdt
!front: For \( f : \mathbb{R}^n \to \mathbb{R} \) and \( c \in \mathbb{R} \), write the strict sublevel set where \( f<c \) in set-builder notation.
!back: \[ \{x \in \mathbb{R}^n : f(x)<c\} \]
~~~

!id: aEs97CgwkR
!front: For \( f : D \to \mathbb{R} \), write the zero set of \( f \) in set-builder notation.
!back: \[ \{x \in D : f(x)=0\} \]
~~~

!id: hseG7dH6uI
!front: For \( f : I \to \mathbb{R} \), where \( I \subseteq \mathbb{R} \) is an interval, write the set of interior critical points where \( f'(x)=0 \).
!back: \[ \{x \in \operatorname{int}(I) : f'(x) \text{ exists and } f'(x)=0\} \]
!extra: This deliberately excludes endpoints and points where \( f' \) is undefined. Some textbooks use "critical point" more broadly to include points where \( f' \) does not exist.
~~~

!id: jO9YQYb6FA
!front: For \( f : D \to \mathbb{R} \), with \( D \subseteq \mathbb{R} \), write the set of points where \( f \) is continuous using explicit quantifiers.
!back:
\[ \left\{a \in D :
    \forall \varepsilon>0,\ \exists \delta>0,\ \forall x \in D,\
    |x-a|<\delta \implies |f(x)-f(a)|<\varepsilon
\right\} \]
~~~

!id: sz3z13ekwB
!front: For a real sequence \( (a_n)_{n=1}^{\infty} \), write in set-builder notation the set of indices where \( a_n \) is positive.
!back: \[ \{n \in \mathbb{N} : a_n>0\} \]
~~~

!id: YRZlGv36yz
!front: Write in set-builder notation: the set of all real sequences that converge to \( 0 \).
!back:
\[ \left\{(a_n)_{n=1}^{\infty} \in \mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{N}} :
    \forall \varepsilon>0,\ \exists N \in \mathbb{N},\ \forall n \ge N,\ |a_n|<\varepsilon
\right\} \]
!extra: \( \mathbb{R}^{\mathbb{N}} \) denotes the set of all real-valued sequences indexed by \( \mathbb{N} \).
~~~

!id: hGtwfqysJc
!front: What is wrong with \( \{x : x^2 < 4\} \) as a precise set definition, and how could it be repaired?
!back:
The universe for \( x \) is missing. For example:
\[ \{x \in \mathbb{R} : x^2<4\} \]
means the interval \( (-2,2) \), while
\[ \{x \in \mathbb{Z} : x^2<4\} \]
means \( \{-1,0,1\} \).
~~~

!id: WjEAGcxhej
!front: What is wrong with \( \{x \in \mathbb{R} : x=y^2\} \) as a precise set definition, and how could it be repaired if the intended set is the nonnegative real numbers?
!back:
The variable \( y \) is free: its allowed values are not specified. A precise repair is:
\[ \{x \in \mathbb{R} : \exists y \in \mathbb{R},\ x=y^2\}. \]
This equals \( [0,\infty) \).
~~~

!id: EpQdwbynGx
!front: What is wrong with \( \{x \in \mathbb{R} : x=1/n,\ n \in \mathbb{N}\} \), and what is a cleaner version?
!back:
The notation mixes a predicate \( x=1/n \) with a declaration \( n \in \mathbb{N} \). A cleaner version binds \( n \) explicitly:
\[ \left\{\frac{1}{n} : n \in \mathbb{N}\right\}. \]
Equivalently:
\[ \{x \in \mathbb{R} : \exists n \in \mathbb{N},\ x=1/n\}. \]
~~~
